**`extractJsonObject`** — defined in `packages/core/src/prompts/index.ts`, immediately after the `JSON_FENCE` constant:

```typescript
const JSON_FENCE = /```(?:json)?[ \t]*\r?\n([\s\S]*?)```/;

/** Models wrap JSON in fences and prose; tolerate that, but tolerate nothing else. */
function extractJsonObject(raw: string): unknown {
  const candidates = [raw.trim()];
  const fenced = JSON_FENCE.exec(raw);
  if (fenced?.[1] !== undefined) candidates.push(fenced[1]);
  const open = raw.indexOf('{');
  const close = raw.lastIndexOf('}');
  if (open >= 0 && close > open) candidates.push(raw.slice(open, close + 1));

  for (const candidate of candidates) {
    try {
      return JSON.parse(candidate);
    } catch {
      // Next shape.
    }
  }
  throw new SummaryContractError('response: no JSON object found in the model reply');
}
```

It tries three candidate shapes — the trimmed raw string, the fenced block, and the `{...}` slice — and throws the `SummaryContractError` ("no JSON object found in the model reply") if none parse. That thrown message is the one that surfaced as the FM-1 §8 contract failures in iter7/iter8.

Worth noting: there's a sibling helper with the same job in `packages/core/src/summarize/contract.ts`, also immediately after its own `JSON_FENCE` copy — `asJsonObject(raw)`, which is lenient (returns `null` on a miss instead of throwing, so the strict parser owns the diagnosis) and is used by `dropTreeOwnedContents` to strip tree-owned `files`/`symbols` fields before parsing. The strict extractor is `extractJsonObject`; the lenient finder is `asJsonObject`.

